.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
node_modules/

# Model bundles are fetched, not committed — see tools/fetch_models.py
models/

# Captured media and derived data never belong in the repo. Participant video
# is identifiable biometric data (REPORT.md §6.4); face blurring alone does not
# de-identify it.
#
# The DERIVED data is covered too, and deliberately so. An extension-based rule
# caught capture.webm but not the session.json and browser_chunks.json beside
# it — and a chunks file is hundreds of thousands of facial-measurement rows,
# which is behavioural biometric data whether or not a face is visible in it.
# Ignoring the container, not the extension, is the rule that actually holds.
data/
sessions/
tempdata/
captures/
*.mp4
*.webm
*.wav
session.json
browser_chunks.json
*.parquet

# AOI definitions are the deliberate exception. They are study CONFIGURATION,
# not participant data — boundaries someone chose to ask a question with — and
# §4.2 makes them part of the method: the same gaze data moved 77%->88%->93%
# purely from refining them. A result is not reproducible without them, so they
# are versionable on purpose while the measurements beside them are not.
!out/aoi_sets/
!out/aoi_sets/*.json

# Codebooks are the same kind of thing: the categories a coder chooses between
# and the moments they are asked about. Configuration, versioned, part of the
# method — a kappa is not interpretable without the definitions it was made
# against.
!out/codebooks/
!out/codebooks/*.json

# The ANNOTATIONS themselves are NOT. A label is a judgement a named coder made
# about a named participant's recording, and the pair is more identifying than
# either half. They stay local, like the measurements they describe.
#
# Spelled out rather than left to the rules above, because nothing above would
# have caught them: the ignores in this section match `*.parquet`,
# `session.json` and `browser_chunks.json` by name, and an annotation file is
# none of those. That is the same extension-versus-container mistake recorded
# further up, one directory over.
out/annotations/

# Stimuli are the deliberate exception to the media rules above. They are part
# of the protocol, not anyone's recording: the study names each one by sha256,
# and a session is not reproducible without the exact file. Only this folder,
# because the extension rules are what keep participant video out of history.
!studies/stimuli/
!studies/stimuli/*.webm
!studies/stimuli/*.mp4

# The pseudonymisation key and the table linking panel IDs to pseudonyms
# (server/pseudonym.py). The link is the "additional information kept
# separately" that makes the rest pseudonymous rather than identified; in the
# repo it would be neither separate nor safe.
out/_keys/

# The deletion log (server/retention.py) holds no participant id, but it is a
# list of which sessions existed and when each was withdrawn — a record about
# participants, and so local like the data it records the removal of.
out/deletions.jsonl

# Playback remuxes are derived copies of participant video, so they fall under
# the same rule as the source — the extension differs, which is exactly the
# gap that let session.json through once already.
*.playback.webm
*.playback.mp4

# Third-party papers, kept locally but not redistributed. The research/*.md
# passes cite each one by title and DOI, so the evidence trail survives without
# the repo carrying someone else's copyrighted PDFs into a public history.
research/sources/
